        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Six real options: epoxy, polyaspartic, paint, interlocking tiles, roll-out matting and penetrating sealer. They split into things that bond to the slab and things that do not, and that division decides more than price or appearance does.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            So start with the concrete. Tape plastic sheeting to the bare slab for 48 hours, and if there is condensation underneath, every bonded coating is eventually going to be pushed off. Check for spalling and structural cracking too, because no covering repairs a slab and several will fail on one that needed repair first.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            On dry, sound concrete, 100% solids epoxy is the best value and polyaspartic buys UV stability and a one-day install. On a damp or cracked slab, tiles and matting stop being a compromise and start being the only thing that works. And on a working garage where the floor is a floor, a penetrating sealer quietly does the useful part for the least money.
          </p>
        </div>


